{"id":"e06caf0c-0674-45fd-b699-7d6c58576cda","object":"chat.completion","created":1785022127,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_dict = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        children.setdefault(n['id'], [])\\n        p = n.get('parent')\\n        if p is not None:\\n            children.setdefault(p, []).append(n['id'])\\n    # Precompute subtree nodes for each node (including itself)\\n    subtree = {}\\n    def get_subtree(nid):\\n        if nid in subtree:\\n            return subtree[nid]\\n        res = {nid}\\n        for c in children.get(nid, []):\\n            res.update(get_subtree(c))\\n        subtree[nid] = res\\n        return res\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        get_subtree(n['id'])\\n    # initial retained set\\n    retained = set(node_dict.keys())\\n    # symbol -> set of node ids\\n    sym_nodes = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        sym_nodes.setdefault(n['symbol'], set()).add(n['id'])\\n    # sort nodes: descending depth, ascending id\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed = []\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if node.get('parent') is None:\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required:\\n            continue\\n        # check retained children\\n        has_retained_child = any(c in retained for c in children.get(nid, []))\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        # subtree currently retained\\n        sub_retained = [x for x in subtree[nid] if x in retained]\\n        # check required symbols not lost\\n        loss = False\\n        for sym in required:\\n            sym_retained = sym_nodes.get(sym, set()) & retained\\n            if not sym_retained:\\n                loss = True\\n                break\\n            # all occurrences in subtree?\\n            if sym_retained.issubset(sub_retained):\\n                loss = True\\n                break\\n        if not loss:\\n            # remove entire subtree\\n            for x in sub_retained:\\n                retained.discard(x)\\n                sym_nodes[node_dict[x]['symbol']].discard(x)\\n            removed.append(nid)\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed, \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_sorted}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that performs a grammar-guided node reduction. The input case has \"nodes\" list with properties: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also \"required_symbols\". The output must be {\"removed_node_ids\": [...], \"retained_node_ids\": [...]}.\n\nThe algorithm: We have a tree (root with parent null). We need to reduce nodes that are removable, but ensure required symbols are all present. The process: iterate nodes in order of depth decreasing (deeper first), and for each node that is removable, check if removing it (along with its subtree?) would preserve required symbols. But careful: The description: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" Also: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we cannot remove a node that has children that are retained. But if we remove a node, its children would also be removed? In typical tree reduction, removing a node means deleting that node and its subtree. However, there is also \"replacement candidates\" mentioned, but not described in detail. The problem says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" but the mechanism boundary restricts to \"Perses syntax-guided reduction on a miniature grammar; parser generation and real compiler execution are excluded.\" So likely we only consider deletion (removal) of nodes. Also \"registered_candidate_order\" - but we don't have candidates explicitly. The description says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" and \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So we need to sort nodes by descending depth, and then by node ID (string) for tie break.\n\nWe need to determine which nodes can be removed. We cannot remove a node if:\n- It is root (parent null)\n- Its \"removable\" is false\n- It is a required symbol? Actually \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" means if the node's symbol is in required_symbols, we cannot remove it.\n- \"or a node with retained children.\" Means if the node has children that are retained, we cannot remove it. But when we process nodes, we need to consider the current state of the tree. Since we process deeper first, children are processed before parents. So when we consider a parent, its children may have been removed already. So we can remove a parent if all its children have been removed (or it has no children). Also we need to ensure that after removal, all required symbols still appear somewhere in the tree. So before removing a node, we check if any required symbol is only present in the subtree of that node. If so, we cannot remove.\n\nWe must also consider that removing a node removes its entire subtree. So the required symbols check: For each required symbol, there must be at least one node in the tree (after removal) that has that symbol. The root is always there.\n\nImplementation steps:\n- Build a dict mapping id->node.\n- Build a list of children for each parent.\n- Determine initial set of required symbols (list).\n- Sort nodes by depth descending, then by node id ascending (string comparison).\n- Initialize retained set: all node ids.\n- Initialize removed list empty.\n- For each node in sorted order:\n   - If node is not removable: skip.\n   - If node is root (parent null): skip.\n   - If node's symbol in required_symbols: skip? Actually \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" means if that node's symbol is required, we cannot remove it. But what if there are multiple nodes with the same required symbol? The phrasing \"a required symbol\" likely means the node directly has a symbol that is in required_symbols. So we skip removal of nodes whose symbol is in required_symbols.\n   - Check if any children are still retained (i.e., in retained set). Because we process deeper first, children may have been removed. So if node has children and any child id is in retained set, then we cannot remove because \"node with retained children\". So skip.\n   - Check if removing this node (and its subtree) would cause any required symbol to disappear. That is, for each required symbol, if the only nodes with that symbol in the entire tree are within the subtree of this node (including itself), then we cannot remove. Since we know the current retained set, we can compute the set of nodes that would be removed: the subtree of this node (all descendants) plus itself. But we have already removed some descendants? Actually we are considering removing the node, but its descendants that are still retained would also be removed. However, because we process deeper first, any descendant that is removable and could be removed would have already been considered. But some descendants might be not removable (e.g., required symbols, or not removable). So we need to check if any required symbol would be lost. So we need to know, for each required symbol, whether there is at least one node outside the subtree that has that symbol.\n   - To do that efficiently: Precompute for each node the set of symbols in its subtree? But we only need to check for required symbols. We can compute for each required symbol the list of nodes that have that symbol (initially). Then when we consider removing a node, we need to check if any required symbol's occurrence set is entirely within the subtree of that node. Since we process deeper first, and we only remove nodes that are removable and not required symbol, the set of occurrences for each required symbol only changes when we remove a node that is the only occurrence? But we are checking before removal. So we can compute for each required symbol the set of node ids that have that symbol. Then for a candidate node, find all descendants (including itself) and check if any required symbol's node set is a subset of that descendant set. If yes, then removal would lose that symbol.\n\nBut careful: descendants that have already been removed are not in the retained set. The \"subtree\" we consider for removal is the set of nodes that are currently retained and are in the subtree (including the node itself). However, if a descendant was already removed, it is not in the retained set, so it won't contribute to required symbols. So the check should be: For each required symbol, is there any retained node (outside the candidate's subtree) that has that symbol? Equivalent to: The set of retained nodes with that symbol minus the subtree set is non-empty.\n\nSo we need to maintain for each required symbol the set of retained nodes that have that symbol. Initially, that's all nodes with that symbol. When we remove a node, we remove that node and its entire subtree (all retained nodes in that subtree) from the retained set and from the symbol sets. So we can update the symbol sets accordingly.\n\nAlgorithm:\n- Build children mapping.\n- Build subtree nodes (all descendants) for each node? Alternatively, when removing, we need to know which nodes are in the subtree. We can compute it on the fly using DFS, but it's inefficient. Better to precompute a list of all nodes in subtree for each node (including itself). Since the tree is small (fixtures have 7 nodes), we can precompute using recursion.\n\n- Initialize retained = set(all node ids)\n- Initialize symbol_to_nodes: dict symbol -> set of node ids (retained) that have that symbol.\n- For each required symbol, it must be present initially (root has \"root\" required, others maybe). We assume valid.\n\n- Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending.\n- removed_ids = []\n- For each node in sorted order:\n   - if node.id not in retained: continue (already removed as part of parent)\n   - if not node.removable: continue\n   - if node.parent is None: continue (root)\n   - if node.symbol in required_symbols: continue (required symbol node)\n   - # check if any children retained\n   - has_retained_child = any(child_id in retained for child_id in children.get(node.id, []))\n   - if has_retained_child: continue\n   - # check if removal would lose a required symbol\n   - # get all nodes in subtree that are still retained\n   - subtree_retained = [nid for nid in subtree_nodes[node.id] if nid in retained]\n   - # For each required symbol, check if any retained node outside subtree_retained has that symbol\n   - can_remove = True\n   - for sym in required_symbols:\n        # nodes with that symbol currently retained\n        sym_nodes = symbol_to_nodes[sym] & retained\n        # nodes in subtree with that symbol\n        sym_subtree = sym_nodes.intersection(subtree_retained)\n        if sym_subtree == sym_nodes:\n            # all occurrences are in subtree, removal would lose symbol\n            can_remove = False\n            break\n   - if can_remove:\n        # Remove the entire subtree\n        for nid in subtree_retained:\n            retained.discard(nid)\n            # update symbol_to_nodes: remove from its symbol's set\n            sym = nodes_dict[nid].symbol\n            symbol_to_nodes[sym].discard(nid)\n        removed_ids.append(node.id)\n        # Note: we append the node id itself. The list should contain removed node ids in order of removal.\n        # But the output requires \"removed_node_ids\" array. According to \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\"\n        # So removed_node_ids should be in the order they were removed (the iteration order, which is descending depth, then id). \n        # We'll collect in that order.\n\n- After processing all nodes, retained_node_ids = sorted(list(retained)) (ascending? \"sorted retained IDs\" means alphabetically sorted? Usually node ids are strings like \"n1\",\"n2\", etc. So we sort them ascending.)\n- removed_node_ids = the list we built (order of removal).\n\n- Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_node_ids, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids}.\n\nCheck with fixtures? We only have public fixtures. We can test mentally on first case.\n\nCase 0 payload:\nnodes: root (depth0, not removable, symbol root), n1 (parent root, depth1, not removable, symbol s1), n2 (parent root, depth1, removable, symbol s2), n3 (parent root, depth1, not removable, symbol s0), n4 (parent n2, depth2, removable, symbol s1), n5 (parent n3, depth2, not removable, symbol s2), n6 (parent n1, depth2, removable, symbol s0)\nrequired_symbols: [\"root\",\"s1\"]\n\nInitial retained: all 7. symbol_to_nodes: root: {root}, s1: {n1, n4}, s2: {n2, n5}, s0: {n3, n6}.\n\nSort by depth descending: depth2: n4, n5, n6. depth1: n1, n2, n3. depth0: root. Then tie-break id: n4, n5, n6; then n1,n2,n3; root.\n\nProcess:\nn4: removable? yes. parent n2 (not root). symbol s1 is required? s1 is in required_symbols, so skip (cannot remove required symbol node). So skip.\nn5: removable? no (false). skip.\nn6: removable? yes. parent n1 (not root). symbol s0 not required. children? n6 has no children? in nodes list, no children. So no retained children. Check required symbols: root and s1. root is not in subtree of n6 (subtree: [n6]). s1 occurrences: n1 and n4. Subtree has none. So removal is safe. So remove n6. Remove subtree: [n6]. retained = all except n6. symbol_to_nodes: remove n6 from s0 set -> s0: {n3}. removed_ids = [n6].\n\nNext process depth1 nodes in order: n1, n2, n3.\nn1: removable? false. skip.\nn2: removable? true. parent root (not root? actually root is parent, but root is not null so condition passes). symbol s2 not required. children: n4 (still retained? yes). So has_retained_child = true, so skip (cannot remove node with retained children). So skip.\nn3: removable? false. skip.\nroot: skip (parent null).\n\nFinal retained: all except n6. retained_node_ids sorted: [n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, root]. removed_node_ids: [n6].\n\nDoes that match expected? Not given. But seems plausible.\n\nSecond case similar. Third case.\n\nWe need to ensure we implement exactly as described. Also \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" might imply we only consider removal of nodes that are removable. The \"removable\" field seems to indicate if the node can be a candidate for removal. Additionally, \"registered_candidate_order\" might refer to the order we process candidates (depth then id). So fine.\n\nEdge cases: What if a node has children but all children have been removed previously? Then has_retained_child false, so it becomes eligible. That's fine.\n\nWe also need to handle the case where a child is not removable but can be removed as part of parent removal? Only if parent is removed, child goes with it. So our algorithm removes entire subtree when parent is removed. That's correct.\n\nWe must not mutate case. We'll create copies.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Build a dict nodes_dict = {n['id']: n for n in case['nodes']}\n- Build children: for each node, if parent not None, add to children[parent]. defaultdict(list)\n- Precompute subtree_nodes: use recursion or stack. For each node, compute set of descendant ids (including itself). Since small, we can do DFS for each node.\n- required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n- Sort nodes list by (depth, id) but descending depth, ascending id. We can use: sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n- Maintain retained set: set of all ids initially.\n- symbol_to_nodes: defaultdict(set). For each node, add id to symbol_to_nodes[node['symbol']].\n- Iterate as above.\n\nReturn result.\n\nNow produce the final answer: a JSON object with \"implementation\" containing Python source as a JSON string. Need to escape properly.\n\nWe'll write the code with triple-quoted strings? In JSON string, we need to escape double quotes, backslashes, newlines as \\n. We'll produce a single line string or use \\n. Safer to produce the source as a string with newlines escaped.\n\nI'll write the Python function solve(case) and then wrap it in JSON.\n\nLet's write the code clearly with proper indentation.\n\nPotential pitfalls: The required_symbols list may have duplicates? Probably not. We treat as set.\n\nAlso note: The root node has id \"root\" and symbol \"root\". required_symbols includes \"root\". So we never remove root.\n\nFor \"node with retained children\" - we check children list. But if a node has children that are not in retained (already removed), it's fine.\n\nTesting on first case: as above, works.\n\nSecond case: nodes similar but utility different, but algorithm doesn't use utility. So result should be same structure: remove n6 only. Check required symbols: s1 appears at n1 (not removable) and n4 (removable). If n4 were to be removed? n4 is removable and symbol s1 is required, so we skip removal of n4 because it's required symbol node. So n4 stays. So no other removal.\n\nThird case: same.\n\nSo for all three public fixtures, removed_node_ids should be [\"n6\"] and retained sorted.\n\nBut is there any scenario where a node with required symbol could be removed if there is another node with same symbol retained? The instructions say \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" - it seems absolute regardless of duplicates. So we skip any node whose symbol is in required_symbols.\n\nThus implementation is straightforward.\n\nNow produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2057,"completion_tokens":4036,"total_tokens":6093,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_toke